## a b s t r a c t
The synergistic effect between transition metal active centers and the generation of multiple removal pathways has a significant impact on the catalytic activation efficiency of peroxymonosulfate. In this work, a kind of composite catalyst was prepared by growing VCo-metal–organic frameworks (VCoMOF) in-situ on the surface of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ by a solvothermal method. The morphology and structure are characterized by Transmission Electron Microscope (TEM), Energy Dispersion Spectrum (EDS), Atomic Force Microscope (AFM), etc. Response surface methodology was used to optimize the experimental conditions. Only $5\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst can be used to effectively activate PMS and remove $96.14\%$ ciprofloxacin (CIP, $20\mathrm{mg}/$ L) within $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . The removal effect of catalyst on CIP in different actual water environment was explored. In addition, the fluorescence spectrum test also verified the effective removal of ciprofloxacin. V-Co-Ti ternary system provides a wealth of active sites for CIP removal. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) and lear sweep voltammetry (LSV) tests showed the existence of the electron transfer pathway. The results of density functional theory (DFT) calculation show that $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ has excellent adsorption and activation ability for PMS. At the same time, the hydrophilicity of the catalyst makes PMS more inclined to react with water molecules, which promotes the formation of a unique superoxide radical path. $\circledcirc$ 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
As a kind of persistent organic pollutants, high level of broadspectrum antibiotics in sewage have raised concerns due to the drug resistance [1]. Ciprofloxacin (CIP), as a kind of fluoroquinolone broad-spectrum antibiotics [2], has been widely used in animal husbandry, fishery, pharmaceutical and other fields [3]. However, it is considered to be carcinogenic and difficult to be biodegraded, which poses a serious threat to human drinking water safety and ecological environment [4,5]. CIP has acute high-dose exposure and are more lethal and sub lethal to some organisms [6]. Methylene blue (MB), rhodamine B (RhB) and tetracycline hydrochloride (TCHCl) are also typical organic pollutants in water. The advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) based on peroxymonosulfate (PMS) emerging in recent years is expected to solve this pollution problem [7]. PMS has strong oxidation ability and excellent stability [8], which is conducive to the application in practical environment. When activated, it produces a variety of free radicals such as ${\mathsf{O}}_{2}\cdot\mathrm{\Lambda}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{\cdot}^{-}$ , and OH, which can effectively participate in the degradation of organic pollutants [9]. Compared with traditional Fenton system based on OH, $S O_{4}$  has the following advantages, such as the oxidation potential of $50_{4}\mathrm{{^{-}}}\left(2.5\mathrm{{-}}3.1\mathrm{{V}}\right.$ vs Normal Hydrogen Electrode) being $0.4\mathrm{-}0.7\:\mathrm{V}$ higher than that of OH $(1.8\mathrm{-}2.7~\mathrm{V}$ vs Normal Hydrogen Electrode), and long half-life $(30-40~\upmu s)$ and high pH range $({\mathsf{p H}}\colon2\substack{-9})$ for application [10,11]. It is considered to be an excellent choice for removing refractory organic pollutants [12].
Unfortunately, PMS has poor oxidation capacity under normal conditions. The oxidation potential of PMS can be stimulated by the activation of physical fields such as ultrasound [13], light and heat [14,15] or catalysis through transition metals [16]. However, there are problems of energy consumption and serious secondary pollution. In recent years, Metal-Organic Framework (MOF) materials have been attracted much attention as efficient catalysts for activation of PMS [17] because of their large specific surface area, accessible active sites and adjustable structure [18]. At the initial stage, PMS can be adsorbed to make it into a more active state [19], and the exposed catalytic active sites can more effectively break the peroxy bond of PMS to produce free radicals [20]. However, due to the complexity of water environment, the high stability and catalytic performance of MOF catalyst are very challenging. In order to further improve the properties of MOF, recent studies have focused on the construction of MOF derivatives [21], multicomponent MOF [22] and MOF composites [23–25]. For example, $\mathsf{C o F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanocrystals derived from Co/Fe bi-MOF have unique structure and ferromagnetic properties, which makes them easy to be separated from liquid phase while effectively activate PMS to degrade Bisphenol A [26]. Similarly, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{C}.$ -porous carbon derived from $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ loaded MOF-74(Zn) showed excellent adsorption and catalytic properties [27]. Inspired by these, our scheme took into account of the construction of bimetallic MOF and its composite with a carrier.
MXene is considered to be one of the best carriers for MOF growth [28]. MXenes are two-dimensional layered transition metal $(\mathsf{M}_{\mathrm{n}+1})$ carbides or nitrides $(\mathsf{X}_{\mathrm{n}})$ produced by selectively etching of element A (mainly referring to the elements of group 13 or 14) from MAX phase $(\mathbb{M}_{\mathrm{n+1}}\mathbb{A}\mathbb{X}_{\mathrm{n}}$ , M represents the early transition metal, and $\mathsf{X}$ represents elements C or N), mainly including $\mathrm{Ti}_{2}\mathsf C$ $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N b}_{2}\mathsf{C}$ and $\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{C}$ , etc [29]. With the increasing mature preparation technology of MXene, researchers have begun to design its composites [30]. There are abundant hydroxyl and fluorine terminal groups on the surface of MXene, which is conducive to the insitu nucleation of MOF [31]. Furthermore, in situ growth on the surface of MXene can effectively prevent the reduction of active surface caused by aggregation in the catalytic process [32]. Recent studies also show the feasibility of MXene composite strategy. The hybrid composites prepared by mixing Ti-MOF precursor solution with MXene liquid using solvothermal method is impressive [33]. MOF derived CoFe-LDH has excellent oxygen evolution reaction properties after compounding with MXene [34].
VCo-MOF has been reported by a few papers [35–37]. VCo-MOF is mostly used in lithium storage anode materials and presents amorphous state. Its application for advanced oxidation technology based on sulfate radical has not been explored. Based on the above material design strategy, we prepared a novel Barnaclelike VCo-MOF in small volume reactors and further delicately synthesized $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ $\mathrm{T}=0$ , F) nano-sheets through an insitu MOF derived strategy. The morphology and structure of VCo$\mathsf{M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ was adjusted by changing the dosage of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ nanosheets and analyzed in detail by XRD, AFM, TEM and other characterization methods. Besides, the V-Co-Ti ternary catalytic system constructed by bimetallic MOF and MXene was comprehensively evaluated by various performance tests of catalytic activating PMS for ciprofloxacin (CIP) removal. In particular, $0_{2}$  plays an important role in the removal of CIP in $\mathrm{VCo-MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}-4/}$ PMS system. The combination of density functional theory (DFT) calculation and characterization test (XPS, contact angle, EPR test and free radical capture experiment) are used to clarify the formation of special superoxide radical path.
## 2. Experimental details
## 2.1. Chemical agents
Cobalt chloride hexahydrate $_\mathrm{CoCl_{2}\cdot6H_{2}O}$ , $99\%$ , SCR), vanadium chloride $\mathrm{\nabla{VCl}}_{3}$ , $97\%$ , Aladdin), 2-aminoterephthalic acid $(\mathsf{C}_{8}\mathsf{H}_{7}\mathsf{N O}_{4}$ $98\%$ Macklin), and titanium aluminum carbide (400 mesh, Enyan new material technology), which were the main reagents for preparing the catalysts. Lithium fluoride (LiF, $99\%$ , Macklin) and concentrated hydrochloric acid (AR, GHTECH) were used as etchant. Methanol (AR, Shanghai Shenbo) and N, Ndimethylformamide (DMF, AR, GHTECH) were used as solutions and detergent for preparing the catalysts. Tetracycline hydrochloride (TC HCl, BR, Macklin), methylene blue (MB, $\geq70.0\ \%$ ， GHTECH), rhodamine B (RhB, $95\%$ , Tianjin Guangfu) and ciprofloxacin hydrochloride monohydrate (CIP, $\geq98\ \%.$ Aladdin) were the simulated organic pollutants. tert-Butyl alcohol (TBA, AR, GHTECH), methanol (AR, Shanghai Shenbo), and L- histidine $298\ \%$ , Aladdin) were as free radical quenchers. Sodium thiosulfate $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ , $99\%$ , GHTECH) was as a terminator.
## 2.2. Preparation of $V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}$
## 2.2.1. Preparation of $T\dot{\iota}_{3}C_{2}T_{x}$
MXene $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}},$ ${\mathrm{T}}=0$ , F) was prepared by HF selective in-situ etching of Al from MAX phase $(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2})$ . Briefly, first, $\overline{{1.62\mathrm{~g~}}}$ $\langle0.06\mathrm{\mol}\rangle$ of LiF was added to the Teflon lined reactor containing
$20~\mathrm{mL}$ of concentrated hydrochloric acid (12 M, $\mathsf{M}=\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{L})$ , and the mixture was stirred well at room temperature in a water bath at $30\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The indirect preparation of hydrofluoric acid (HF) by LiF $+\mathsf{H C l}$ is much safer, and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ has the function of intercalation, which can realize the simultaneous etching and intercalation [38]. Then, $\begin{array}{r}{1\ \mathrm{~g~Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}}\end{array}$ was added to the reactor and stirred at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to achieve sufficient etching and intercalation. After etching, the solution was centrifuged at $3500~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $5~\mathrm{min}$ and the liquid waste containing HF was carefully removed into the waste liquid tank, then the black precipitation was washed with $1\mathrm{~M~}$ hydrochloric acid for 3 times and ultrapure water for several times until the pH close to 7. The washed sample was transferred into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ glass bottle, then $40~\mathrm{mL}$ ultrapure water was added, and after tightening the bottle cap, it was treated by ultrasonic for $^\mathrm{~2~h~}$ . Finally, the obtained mixture was centrifuged at $3500\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ , and the supernatant was collected that a dark green highly concentrated $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ colloidal solution was obtained. As usual [39], $5~\mathrm{mL}$ MXene solution was dried under vacuum at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to remove the solvent, and finally the obtained product was used to calculate the concentration with the value of $11~\mathrm{mg}$ MXene $/\mathrm{mL}$ obtained. The preparation flow chart is shown in Fig. S1.
## 2.2.2. Preparation of VCo-MOF
The synthesis method of VCo-MOF is described as below. $0.119\mathrm{g}\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}$ $0.5\mathrm{mmol})$ and $0.049\mathrm{g}\mathrm{VCl}_{3}$ $\cdot0.3\mathrm{mmol})$ are dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF at the same time and stirred vigorously for $15\mathrm{min}$ . Then, $0.145\mathrm{~g~}$ aminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC, ligand, $0.8\mathrm{\mmol}$ was ultrasonically dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF. The above raw materials are added according to the set proportion. The ligand solution was slowly dropped into the metal ion solution under the condition of stirring for $15\mathrm{min}$ , followed by addition of $4~\mathrm{mL}$ ultrapure water and continue stirred for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . Finally, the uniform gray black solution was equally divided into three $10~\mathrm{mL}$ reactors and reacted at $180^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Compared with the large volume reactor, the small volume reactor $\mathrm{\Omega}_{\mathrm{\cdot}10\mathrm{\mL}}$ has higher pressure, which is conducive to the growth of crystals. The product was washed with methanol and DMF for several times and dried under vacuum at 60 $\mathrm{{}^{\circ}C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to obtain VCo-MOF black powder. The preparation flow chart is shown in Fig. S2.
## 2.2.3. Preparation of $V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}$
The steps and details of the experiment are very similar with the preparation of VCo-MOF that the details are given in Fig. S3. The preparation process of $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}$ was schematically shown in Fig. 1. Significantly, the step of adding water is replaced by adding 0.1, 1, 4, 10 mL $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ solution (set $\mathsf{a s}\times\mathsf{m L}$ , and the amount of DMF $((24-\mathbf{x})/2\ \mathrm{mL})$ added to the ligand solution is increased or reduced accordingly to maintain the total volume of $24~\mathrm{mL}$ . In addition, ultrasonic treatment for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ is required before loading into $10~\mathrm{mL}$ lined reactor. The product was washed with methanol and DMF for several times and dried under vacuum at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
## 2.3. Characterization
In addition, the catalyst performance evaluation method, electrochemical test method and DFT calculation method are given in the Supporting materials in details.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of VCo-MOF@Ti3C2Tx
Herein, the as-prepared VCo-MOF in a small volume reactor presents regular dodecahedron and special barnacle like hollow structure (Fig. 2a-d and Fig. S4), which is very different from the amorphous distorted morphology reported previously [35]. The reason for the unique morphology may be that the small volume reactor has a higher-pressure during synthesis. In our early exploration, VCo-MOF with unique morphology has high catalytic activity but poor stability in PMS system that V in VCo-MOF is easily dissolved in the liquid phase with light yellow color after catalysis (Fig. S5). Luckily, most of VCo-MOF-180 remains after catalysis while other samples prepared at lower temperature dissolved, so $180^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ is selected for the following experiments.
Fortunately, the strong interaction between organic ligands in VCo-MOF structure and MXene surface functional groups can improve the stability of the composites $(\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{X}})$ [40]. The morphology and structural characteristics of MXene and $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{X}}$ were analyzed by SEM, TEM and AFM. In Fig. 2e, the MAX phase raw material presents a block state, which is the alternating close fit of titanium carbide and aluminum layer. The MAX phase was etched and exfoliated to obtain thin $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ layers (Fig. 2f).
When the dosage of MXene solution is $0.1~\mathrm{mL}$ , it can be clearly seen in Fig. 2g that barnacle like hollow VCo-MOF (Fig. 2d) grows by in-situ on MXene layer. When the dosage of MXene solution increased to $1~\mathrm{mL}$ , the overall morphology was the irregular hybrid state of MXene and MOF (Fig. 2h). This may be due to the compression of MOF growth space caused by the increase of MXene concentration, and then be inhibited unevenly. When the dosage of MXene solution is high, MOF is difficult to grow further after nucleation on the surface of MXene, and the stacking between MXene layers may be caused by MOF as a bridge (Fig. 2i).
As shown in Fig. S4 and Table S1, the distribution of V, Co, N, C and O indicates the successful preparation of VCo-MOF with unique morphology. The elemental mapping images depict that C, N, O, F, Ti, V and Co elements (Fig. 2k-q) are all distributed uniformly within the $\mathrm{VCo-MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\mathrm{-}4$ structure (Fig. 2j). Fig. 2r shows the element distribution spectrum. The uniform distribution of V, Co and N on MXene lamellae proves that VCo-MOF can grow by rich functional groups on its surface.
TEM test can further clarify the in-situ growth of VCo-MOF on the surface of MXene (Fig. 3). As shown in Fig. 3a-c, dark circular areas (white dashed circle in the Fig. 3c) are superimposed or randomly distributed on the surface of MXene thin layer. Then, a small area with obvious contrast was selected for mapping (Fig. 3e-k). The uniform distribution of C, N, O, Co, V, F and Ti elements can be observed from the images, and Co, V, F and other elements can be obviously observed in the raised area (MOF growth area).
The concentration distribution difference of F may be because the F-terminal group is similar to –OH, which promotes the nucleation and growth of MOF, then MOF tends to grow in the Fterminal groups rich region. The selected area electron diffraction in Fig. 3l shows that the sample is polycrystalline.
AFM was used to study the surface roughness (Fig. 3m-n) and thickness (Fig. S6) of the sample. The thickness of the layer is measured by AFM built-in software for three times and averaged. The results showed that the surface roughness increased after MOF growing in-situ on the MXene surface, and the thickness increased from $11.67~\mathrm{nm}$ to $15.67\ \mathrm{nm}$ (non-uniform), reflecting the partial asymmetric folding and damage of MXene under solvothermal conditions.
X-ray diffraction (XRD) and laser pen were used to verify whether MAX phase powder was transformed into ultra-thin layered $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ (Fig. 4a). Strong diffraction peaks correspond to MAX phase (black line). The broadening of the corresponding peak in $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ (red line) is caused by the loss of out of plane diffraction signal by exfoliation and the nano lamellar structure [41], so the intensity of diffraction peak decreases. The shift of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{X}}$ peak to low angle and the Tyndall effect after laser passing through the solution all prove the transition from $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathsf{A l C}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\bf x}$ .
The changes of XRD patterns caused by different $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ addition during the catalyst’s preparation is shown in Fig. 4b. The characteristic peak intensity of VCo-MOF decreases with the increase of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ addition, indicating that the in-situ growth of VCo-MOF is gradually inhibited.
In order to further demonstrate the successful growth of VCoMOF on MXene surface, Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) test was carried out (Fig. 4c). Specifically, $3429~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the characteristic peak of $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ in ligand [42]. The peaks at 3321, 1311 and $1242\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are the characteristic peaks belonging to CAN stretching of the benzenoid ring [43–45]. The range of 1390 to $1545~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be designated as ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}0{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ stretching [46], and 1622, 1593 and $812~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represent $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ vibrations [47], –COOH [48] and ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ vibrations [44] respectively. The above peaks exhibit the coordination of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC ligand with metal ions or clusters in MOF structure. With the increase of MXene dosage, the characteristic peaks related to MOF gradually become less obvious, which is consistent with the results of XRD. In particular, there is almost no characteristic peak related to VCo-MOF in the XRD spectrum of $\mathtt{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\mathtt{-4}$ , but it can be obviously observed in the FTIR results, which indicates that VCo-MOF can still grow at this MXene dosage.
The $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherm was applied to analyze the Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface areas (Fig. 4d). All curves in the figure have the shape similar to type IV isotherm, which shows that the adsorbent has porous and microporous characteristics. The hysteresis loop of isotherm can match H3 hysteresis loop, indicating that the pore structure is very irregular [49]. This is consistent with the state of random growth of VCo-MOF on MXene surface in TEM results (Fig. 3). Combined with Table S2 for analysis, the $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ of VCo-MOF is small $(16.37~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ . Channel connection or metal plug may lead to a sudden drop in $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ , which may be a feature of bimetallic MOF [35,50]. VCo$\mathrm{MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}1$ has the largest $\mathrm{S_{BET}}\left(150.72~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ , which probably related to its special hybrid structure (Fig. 2f). The spontaneous stacking of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\mathrm{x}}$ nanosheets may lead to a significant reduction in $S_{\mathrm{BET}}$ when the dosage of MXene is high.
## 3.2. Interaction and optimization of experimental parameters
## 3.2.1. Optimization of condition parameters by response surface method
The results of previous exploration show that the CIP removal effect is the best when the dosage of MXene is $4~\mathrm{mL}$ (Fig. S7).
Response surface method (RSM) is an effective method for experimental conditions optimization by analyzing the interaction between various factors to predict or determine the best combination conditions, so as to achieve the best experimental results.
Under the conditions of three factors and three levels (Table 1), the whole experiment was designed by using the Box–Behnken design (BBD) method by Design-Expert 8.0.6 software. 17 groups of experiments (Table S3) were designed by the software after importing the factors (i.e., three factors and three levels) to comprehensively explore the effects of initial pH, PMS dosage and catalyst dosage on CIP removal performance. When $5\mathrm{mg}$ catalyst and ${90}~\mathrm{mg}$ PMS were added into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ CIP solution at $\mathsf{p H}5.5$ , the removal efficiency reaches the optimum $(96.14\%)$ . Compared with the catalysts reported in other literatures (Table S4), VCo$\mathrm{MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}–4$ can achieve better CIP removal effect under the conditions of low dosage.
The experimental results were analyzed by polynomial regression with CIP removal efficiency (E) as the response value. As shown in Eq. (1):
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{E=93.02\ +\ 6.02A\ +\ 2.838-0.74C\ +\ 0.012A\mathrm{{B}}-0.85\mathrm{{AC}}}}\\ {{+\ 0.14{\mathrm{BC}}-7.66A^{2}-0.59{\mathrm{B}}^{2}-1.40\mathrm{{C}}^{2}}}\end{array}
$$
$\mathbf{\dot{A}}=$ initial $\mathsf{p H}$ value, $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~B~}}=$ dosage of PMS, $C=$ dosage of VCo$\mathrm{MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}–4\rangle$ .
After determining the relationship between the corresponding coefficients and the response surface, the reliability of the polynomial is further verified by ANOVA analysis (Table S5). According to the experimental results, the F-value (37.55) is much higher than 1 and the p-value (less than0.0001) is lower than the significance level of 0.05, which proves that this model is suitable for the data analysis [51]. In addition, the values of R squared and adj Rsquared are 0.9797 and 0.9536 respectively, illustrating that the polynomial Eq. (1) can effectively simulate the actual response surface. The experimental data points fit well with the simulation line (Fig. S8), which also reflects the perfect fit between the predicted value and the actual value. The F-values corresponding to the three factors are sorted as follows: initial solution pH values (A, $156.14)>$ dosage of PMS (B, 34.6) $>$ dosage of catalyst (C, 2.38), which indicates that pH value and PMS dosage are the main influencing factors of removal efficiency. The significant effect of initial pH value is mainly due to the different control effects of unsaturated Lewis acid center and F-Ti end group on PMS decomposition under different pH conditions [52]. Based on RSM analysis (Fig. 5), the optimized experimental conditions were as follows: pH value was 5.5, PMS dosage was $60~\mathrm{mg/100~mL}$ and catalyst dosage were $10~\mathrm{mg/100~mL}$ .
## 3.2.2. Effects of various factors on removal efficiency
CIP is distributed from $\mathrm{{ng/L}}$ to $\mathrm{mg/L}$ in surface water, groundwater, and even up to $40~\mathrm{mg/L}$ in some medical wastewater [53]. Moreover, it may cause the residue of drug resistance genes [54].
It is important to explore the methods and environmental conditions for removing CIP, MB, RhB, TC HCl and other organic pollutants. In addition, CIP is the most difficult to remove among these pollutants.
The effect of CIP concentration on the removal efficiency was studied in Fig. 6a. The removal efficiencies of $20{\mathrm{~mg~CIP/L}}$ and $40{\mathrm{~mg~CIP/L}}$ were both over $92.4~\%$ . Considering $40~\mathrm{mg~CIP/L}$ is almost the highest concentration in CIP contaminated water, so it was selected as the targeted concentration to evaluate the degradation performance in the following research [53]. As shown in Fig. 6b, CIP has conjugated benzene ring, cyclopropyl, fluoro, carboxyl and other structures. The fluorescence effect brought by conjugated groups and rigid structure makes the removal effect of CIP can also be explored by fluorescence spectrum [55]. The fluorescence value F was measured at $\lambda_{\mathrm{ex}}=275\mathrm{nm}$ and $\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=441\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 6c), and the decrease of the intensity at $\lambda_{\mathrm{em}}=441\ \mathrm{nm}$ with time shows that the structure of CIP is gradually destroyed.
As shown in Fig. 6d, the effect of MXene dosage during the catalyst preparation on the CIP removal performance was explored. When only PMS is added, the removal efficiency is less than $20\%$ . Samples with MXene dosage less than $1~\mathrm{mL}$ suffers instability in water (dotted line represents instability). When the MXene dosage is $4~\mathrm{mL}$ , the performance is the best, and the CIP removal efficiency can reach $92.4\ \%$ in $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ . Compared with other catalysts (Table S4), $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\mathbf{-}4$ can also achieve excellent performance even at high CIP concentration. The fitting of reaction rate constant (Fig. S9) showed that the removal rate was higher in the first $5\mathrm{min}$ $\mathrm{'K}1_{\mathrm{obs}}=0.061$ ). With the increase of MXene dosage to $10~\mathrm{mL}$ , the performance is not significantly improved. The reason why CIP removal efficiency does not continue to increase with MXene dosage probably is that excessive MXene seriously inhibits the growth of VCo-MOF.
In addition, the efficiencies of $\mathrm{VCo-MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\mathrm{-}4$ catalytic activation of PMS to remove a variety of organic pollutants were also explored (Fig. 6e). The removal efficiency of MB and RhB can reach $99.1\ \%$ and $100\%$ respectively in $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . Besides, $98\%$ of TCHCl can also be effectively removed in $20~\mathrm{\min}$ . VCo$\mathrm{MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}–4$ showed excellent adsorption performance for MB, which is contributed to the electrostatic interaction between MXene surface (negative charge) and MB molecule (cation) [56].
Inorganic anions widely exist in water environment, which may affect the activation effect of catalyst on PMS through free radical capture, changing pH conditions and occupying active sites [57]. Fig. 6f exhibits the effect of different concentrations (1, 5, $10~\mathrm{mM},$ of $C0_{3}^{2-}$ , $\subset{}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ on CIP removal efficiency. $\subset{}^{-}$ , ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ have no significant effect on the CIP removal efficiency, while $C0_{3}^{2-}$ seriously hinders the removal process. This may be because $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ captures ${\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}{\cdot}^{-}$ and generates ${\mathsf{C O}}_{3}$  with lower activity [58], which reduces the removal efficiency of CIP. The possible reaction equations are as follows.
$$
\mathrm{CO_{3}}^{2-}+\mathrm{SO_{4}}\mathrm{-\}\mathrm{SO_{4}}^{2-}+\mathrm{CO_{3}}^{-}
$$
Different water bodies also affect the removal effect of CIP (Fig. S10). Seawater have a great impact on CIP removal by VCo$\mathrm{MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}–4$ activating PMS, which may be due to the interference of anions (Cl-, $S O_{4}^{2-}$ , Br-, ${\mathrm{HCO}}_{3}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ ), cations $(\mathsf{N a}^{+},\mathsf{K}^{+},\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ , $\mathbf{Mg}^{2+}.$ or other pollutants [59].
The recycling performance of the catalyst will increase the possibility of its practical application. After three cycles, its CIP removal performance decreased only from $91.20\mathrm{~\%~}$ to $85.99\ \%$ The performance of the 5th cycle is reduced by $19.9\%$ (Fig. 5g-h), which is similar to that of Cu-Fe-Mn ternary catalyst [60]. The MOF and MXene surfaces are inevitably partially damaged when exposed to the high oxidizing environment caused by PMS, which is probably the main reason for the performance degradation during the cycle. The above results show that $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\mathbf{-}4$ has good reusability. On the conditions of catalyst $=10\mathrm{mg}$ , ${\mathrm{CIP}}=10~{\mathrm{mL}}$ $(40\mathrm{\mg/L})$ , $\mathrm{PMS}\ =\ 60\ \mathrm{\mg}/100\ \mathrm{\mL}$ , $\mathsf{p H}=5.51$ , ICP-AES analysis showed that the leaching amounts of Co, Ti and $\mathsf{V}$ in the catalyst were $0.98~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ , $1.75\mathrm{\mg/L}$ and $0.67~\mathrm{\mg/L}$ respectively. Lower leaching amount of metal ions means less secondary pollution. By comparing the XRD spectra of fresh and used catalyst (Fig. S11), the slight left shift of the low angle peaks and the decrease of the intensity of each peak may be due to the dispersion of the catalyst layer in water and the oxidation of the surface by PMS.
## 3.3. Mechanism analysis
## 3.3.1. Electrochemical analysis
Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) and Cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests are intended to investigate the electron transfer performance in $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}–4/\mathsf{P M S}$ system. In the LSV test, the workstation recorded the electrochemical oxidation current under the conditions of $\mathrm{VCo-MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\mathrm{-}4.$ $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\mathbf{-}4\mathbf{\Lambda}+\mathbf{\Delta}\mathsf{P M S}$ and $\mathrm{VCo-MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}-4+P M S+C I P}$ respectively (Fig. 6i). After adding PMS (red line), the current value increases slightly than that of the catalyst alone (black line), indicating that there is electron transfer between PMS and the catalyst [61]. The oxidation of CIP itself contributes a lower current (green line). Under the conditions of VCo$\mathrm{MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}-4+P M S+C I P}$ (blue line), the current value increases greatly, which proves that the transfer of electrons from VCo$\mathsf{M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}–4/\mathsf{P M S}$ system to CIP [27]. This phenomenon is mainly due to the electron transfer of reactive oxygen species produced by the system to pollutants. Part of the electrons transferred in $\mathrm{VCo-MOF@Ti_{3}C_{2}T_{x}-4/P M S}$ system may come from the shuttle electrons provided by $0_{2}$  [62]. The electron transfer of the composite catalyst itself promotes the conversion of $0_{2}$ or ${\mathsf{H S O}}_{5}^{-}$ adsorbed on the surface into superoxide radicals, which continue to participate in the removal of CIP through electron transfer. In general, electron transfer promotes the non-radical pathway and is conducive to the removal of pollutants. The results of CV test (Fig. S12) are consistent with LSV.
## 3.3.2. DFT calculation
The adsorption and activation behavior of PMS on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{V}{\mathsf{-C o@T i_{3}C_{2}O_{2}}}$ surface were simulated by density functional theory (DFT). The ratio of V and Co in DFT modeling is 1: 1, and their density of states (DOS) were calculated.
Theoretical modeling and structural optimization of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}0_{2}$ and $\mathsf{V}{\mathsf{-C o@T i_{3}C_{2}O_{2}}}$ are carried out (Fig. 7a-d). VCo-MOF is simplified to $\mathsf{V}$ and Co atoms for subsequent calculation. Firstly, the $_{0-0}$ bond length $(\mathrm{l}_{0^{-}0})$ of PMS is calculated. The theoretical $_{0-0}$ bond length of free PMS is $1.326\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ [63]. After PMS adsorbed by $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}$ - $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ (Fig. S13a), its $_{0-0}$ bonds are significantly stretched $(\mathrm{l}_{\mathrm{0-0}}=1$ . $396\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ , indicating that PMS tends to be activated. The tensile effect of $\mathsf{V}{\mathsf{-C o@T i_{3}C_{2}O_{2}}}$ on the $_{0-0}$ bond of PMS is more obvious (Fig. S13b, $\mathrm{l}_{\scriptscriptstyle{0-0}}=1.467\mathrm{~\AA}^{\mathrm{~~}}$ , illustrating that the in-situ growth of VCo-MOF is conducive to the adsorption and activation of PMS.
Furthermore, the adsorption energy of the model is calculated (Fig. 7a, c). The adsorption energy of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ model $\mathrm{(E_{ads}~=~-~}$ $1.044\:\mathrm{eV})$ is much lower than that of $\mathsf{V}{\mathsf{-C o@T i_{3}C_{2}O_{2}}}$ model $\mathrm{{\cal{E}}}_{\mathrm{ads}}=-\mathrm{\boldmath~\Omega~}$ $5.277\ \mathrm{eV})$ , which also proves that $\mathsf{V}{\mathsf{-C o@T i_{3}C_{2}O_{2}}}$ model is more conducive to the adsorption and activation of PMS. The adsorption energy of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ model for PMS can reach $-4.24\mathrm{eV}$ [64], which can clarify the importance of $-\mathsf{O H}$ for PMS adsorption.
The theoretical density of states (DOS) spectrum is drawn by broadening the intensity around each energy eigenvalue. DOS (Fig. 7e-f) presents the information about the states occupied by C, O, Ti, V and Co atoms and their corresponding valence shells at different energy intervals. The total density of states shows that the peak intensity of spin up is almost equal to that of spin down, indicating that $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}0_{2}$ and $\mathsf{V}{\mathsf{-C o@T i_{3}C_{2}O_{2}}}$ theories are nonmagnetic. DOS calculation results show that both $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $V-$ $\mathsf{C o@T i_{3}C_{2}O_{2}}$ exhibit metal behavior [65], and the conductivity of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}0_{2}$ is retained after VCo-MOF growth.
## 3.3.3. XPS and contact angle analysis
In order to study the electronic structure of ${\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}}-$ 4 composites and the valence changes before and after reaction, Xray photoelectron spectra (XPS analysis was carried out. The peak fitting results of C 1 s, O 1 s, Co 2p, Ti 2p, V 2p and N 1 s are shown in Fig. 8. The peaks at 284.78, 286.28 and 288.78 eV correspond to $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C},$ $\mathsf{\Lambda}_{=\mathsf{C},\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{N},\mathsf{C}=0}$ bonds of $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -BDC ligands [66] (Fig. 8a), which also corresponds to the peaks at 1622, 1311, $1545~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in FTIR. The C 1 s peak of the $\mathsf{V C o-M O F@T i_{3}C_{2}T_{x}}\mathbf{-}4$ did not change significantly after use. O 1 s can be deconvoluted into four peaks located at 529.98, 531.18, 531.98 and 533.18 eV respectively (Fig. 8b), belonging to $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}{-0}$ , C-Ti- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot0_{\bf x}$ , C-Ti- $\left(0\mathrm{H}\right)_{\mathrm{x}}$ and C-Ti- $(\mathrm{{H}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{\mathrm{{ads}}})_{\mathrm{{x}}}$ [67]. The presence of C-Ti- $\cdot(\mathrm{OH})_{\mathrm{x}}$ and C-Ti- $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{ads}})_{\mathrm{x}}$ indicates that the sample surface is rich in hydroxyl and is hydrophilicity. The water contact angle of fresh samples is $49.7^{\circ}$ (Fig. 8g), which also reflects the feature of hydrophilicity. The increase of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ -O corresponding peak after use is due to the strong oxidation of PMS.
The valence state of Ti in the catalyst is complex and can be fitted into four pairs of peaks (Fig. 8c). 455.08, 456.38, 461.08 and $462.28\ \mathrm{eV}$ are referred to as Ti peaks in low valence state (e.g. Ti+, $\mathrm{Ti}^{2+}$ ) [68]. The peaks at 459.08 and 464.98 eV should be identified to $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ , while 458.38 and $463.88\ \mathrm{eV}$ should be ascribed to $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}[69]$ . After the catalyst used, the content of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ increased by $30.25\ \%$ , while the low valence Ti almost disappeared. The active Ti species on the surface was partially transformed into super hydrophilic $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with the contact angle in Fig. 8h close to $0^{\circ}$ . As shown in Fig. 8d, the peaks at 785.73 and $801.76\ \mathrm{eV}$ belong to $C o^{2+}$ , and 781.43 and 791.08 eV are attributed to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ , and 789.2 and 804.62 eV are attributed by the satellites of Co [70]. After the catalytic reaction, the content of $C0^{2+}$ decreased by $39.46\ \%$ and that of $C0^{3+}$ increased $39.46\ \%$ meantime, which proves that Co ions participate in the activation of PMS through electron transfer. Furthermore, In Fig. 8e, the content of $\mathsf{V}^{4+}$ (515.88 and 523.28 eV) decreased by $51.25\mathrm{~\%~}$ and $\mathsf{V}^{5+}$ (517.18 and $524.68\ \mathrm{eV},$ ) increased by 51.25 %[71]. The increase of –NH- $^{+}$ content after reaction (Fig. 8f) can be attributed to the electron donor effect of $-\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , which also promotes the catalytic activation of PMS.
